/* ================= PSC content ================= */
const B_ABS="Abstract retrieved", B_KNOW="Abstract not retrieved: based on my general knowledge of the text", B_PART="Partial abstract retrieved";
const PSC=[
{id:"p1",n:1,theme:1,title:"Perspectives on sustainability",who:"Shaun Smith",slides:true,
 lecture:["From 1962–72 (the foundational decade) to Brundtland (1987): sustainable development meets present needs without compromising future generations. Its aim was political (consensus), with not absolute limits but limits of current technology and social organisation. It remained, in essence, a pro-growth agenda. Today: planetary boundaries (Rockström, Steffen, Richardson).",
 "Weak vs strong sustainability: weak (neo-classical, Solow) assumes natural and other capitals are substitutable; strong assumes they are complementary, not interchangeable (planetary boundaries). The conventional three-pillar/Venn view is from Purvis et al.",
 "Dilemma: 'sufficiently vague'. Daly: sustainable development was left vague on purpose to build consensus; growth (getting bigger) ≠ development (getting better); three rules (renewables not faster than regrowth; non-renewables not faster than substitutes; waste not faster than absorption). Swyngedouw (2010): sustainability as post-political: 'impossible not to support', can be captured by incumbents, and it closes off the political question of which natures we want.",
 "Strategies (Huber, via Rudolf & Schmidt 2025): efficiency (tech gains per unit), sufficiency ('seeking enough when more is possible', Princen), consistency (qualitative change in industrial metabolism, mimicking natural cycles). Social sustainability is neglected and messy: Vallance et al. (2011) name development, bridge and maintenance sustainability.",
 "Næss (2001): the first paper on sustainable development in urban planning. Planning orthodoxy (communicative turn) can neglect substantive content; he is somewhat sympathetic to the compact city; argues for per-capita degrowth of building stock; the city in ecology (non-local consequences). Form vs process (Neuman): 'the primacy of process over form'."],
 man:{cite:"Næss, P. (2001). Urban Planning and Sustainable Development. European Planning Studies 9(4), 503–524.",status:"full",
  one:"Truly sustainable urban development needs far more ambitious policy than today, and continuous growth of the building stock in rich countries is incompatible with it. So planning must hold substantive long-term goals and build alliances with supporters of equity and environmental values, rather than seek consensus with everyone.",
  summary:["'If sustainability is everything, maybe it's nothing?' Procedural planning theory is detached from substance; for sustainability that is inappropriate, because suitable procedures depend on the goals (bottom-up works if local lifestyles fit the goals, not if they are the problem).",
  "Substantively: limit energy use, pollution and loss of natural and arable land. Re-use urban areas and densify (compact city) as a strategy, but continuous growth of floor space per capita undermines it, so per-capita building stock must stop growing in wealthy countries (Brundtland's global equity).",
  "Procedurally: use knowledge of environmental consequences and long-term goals, but not only means-ends rationality. He criticises consensus-based communicative planning (a consensus criterion for truth is problematic for environmental knowledge) and proposes scenarios and alliance-building among groups that support the basic equity and environmental values."],
  concepts:[["Substantive vs procedural planning theory","What planning should achieve vs how it should proceed; Næss wants substance back."],["Compact city","Densification/re-use to save land and reduce travel; a means, not a guarantee."],["Per-capita degrowth of building stock","Growth in floor space cancels out efficiency gains."],["Alliance-building","Coalitions with supporters of sustainability values instead of all-stakeholder consensus."]],
  quotes:["\"Rather than aiming at consensus including all stakeholder groups, planning for sustainability should facilitate alliance-building among those population groups who can support the basic equity and environmental values of a sustainable development.\""]},
 fig:`<svg viewBox="0 0 620 200" role="img" aria-label="Weak versus strong sustainability"><g font-size="12"><text x="150" y="20" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="700">Weak sustainability</text><text x="470" y="20" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="700">Strong sustainability</text>
 <circle cx="110" cy="100" r="46" class="bxi"/><circle cx="190" cy="100" r="46" class="bxa"/><circle cx="150" cy="150" r="46" class="bxg"/><text x="95" y="96" font-size="11">economy</text><text x="190" y="96" font-size="11">society</text><text x="130" y="170" font-size="11">nature</text><text x="150" y="196" text-anchor="middle" class="mut" font-size="11">capitals substitutable (Solow)</text>
 <circle cx="470" cy="105" r="80" class="bxg"/><circle cx="470" cy="118" r="52" class="bxa"/><circle cx="470" cy="130" r="26" class="bxi"/><text x="470" y="42" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11">nature / planetary boundaries</text><text x="470" y="84" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11">society</text><text x="470" y="134" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11">economy</text><text x="470" y="196" text-anchor="middle" class="mut" font-size="11">nested: not interchangeable</text></g></svg>`,cap:"Three-pillar Venn (conventional, Purvis et al.) vs nested strong sustainability.",
 rec:[{cite:"Purvis, Mao & Robinson (2019). Three pillars of sustainability: in search of conceptual origins. Sustainability Science 14.",basis:B_ABS,abs:"The three-pillar (social–economic–environmental) Venn model is everywhere, but it has no single origin. It emerged gradually from social and ecological critiques of growth plus UN efforts to reconcile growth with social and environmental concerns (Barbier 1987 drew an early three-system diagram). 'Nowhere have we found a theoretically rigorous description of the three pillars', which frustrates rigorous operationalisation.",slides:"L1: the source of the 'conventional view' slide, and the quote that sustainability is 'an open concept with myriad interpretations and context-specific understanding'."},
 {cite:"Rudolf & Schmidt (2025). Efficiency, sufficiency and consistency in sustainable development. Ecological Economics 227.",basis:B_ABS,abs:"Humanity's dilemma: meeting human needs endangers the natural basis of life. The three strategies are poorly distinguished, so people disagree about which to pursue. A graphical classification by effect on consumption and environmental impact shows the limited long-run effects of efficiency and sufficiency, and the promise of consistency as systemic change (energy transition, circular economy).",slides:"L1: three slides define the strategies (from Joseph Huber). Efficiency = resource/energy gains per unit via technology; sufficiency = 'self-limitation of material needs and an egalitarian distribution', 'seeking enough when more is possible' (Princen 2005); consistency = qualitative change in industrial metabolism via fundamental innovations."}],
 exam:"Comprehension: define weak vs strong sustainability, or the three strategies. Essay: 'Is sustainable development too vague to guide planning?' Use Daly, Swyngedouw (post-political), Purvis (no rigorous theory) and Næss (substance + alliances)."},

{id:"p2",n:2,theme:1,title:"Are cities sustainable?",who:"Shaun Smith",slides:true,
 lecture:["Facts: cities use ~70% of resources and energy, emit ~70% of CO₂ and produce 75–80% of waste; 60%+ lie on coasts; 1.6bn people live in inadequate housing (1bn in slums). Inequality is higher in urban areas (Gini higher in 36 of 42 countries). Domestic Material Consumption grows faster than population; cities' share rises from 50% (2010) to 60% (2050). Shrinkage is a new challenge too.",
 "Definitions vary (Mega & Pederson; Hamilton et al.; Wu 2014: 'an adaptive process… ecosystem services and human well-being'), and measurement is weak: only ~80 cities have consistent CO₂ data (World Bank). Tanguay et al.: indicators suffer from vague definitions, no standard classification, and data limits.",
 "Pessimistic view: cities cause unsustainability; planning is geared to growth (corridors, entrepreneurialism, competition); injustices linger (climate gentrification, Miami); an implementation gap; McPhearson's 'dystopian Anthropocene'; radical shifts are needed. Optimistic view: urbanisation as a 'positive force for transformation' (UN-Habitat); economic (agglomeration, innovation), political (C40, mayors), organisational (bypass national blockages) and conceptual (systems) arguments; compact cities, 15-minute city; big low-cost emission cuts in buildings and transport.",
 "Angelo & Wachsmuth: why does everyone think cities can save the planet? Because of sprawl, informal settlements and climate change. The urban agenda moved from the MDGs (Target 7D, slums) to the universal SDGs with a standalone urban goal (SDG 11). Limits: Krähmer (degrowth critique). Conclusions: cities are disproportionately unsustainable; urban form matters but depends on income and commuting; is absolute sustainability impossible, and do we need a relative conception?"],
 man:{cite:"Angelo, H. & Wachsmuth, D. (2020). Why does everyone think cities can save the planet? Urban Studies 57(11), 2201–2221.",status:"full",
  one:"Global policy flipped from 'the city as sustainability problem' to 'the city as sustainability solution'. This rests on three developments rooted in the 1970s and intensified in the 1990s: reactions to sprawl (North), to informal settlements (South, UN-Habitat 1978) and to climate change (connecting both).",
  summary:["The 'urban sustainability' consensus now looks commonsensical, even inevitable, and is tied to entrepreneurial, competitive urban governance. Urban planning and sustainability planning are merging.","Three roots: (1) sprawl: the Global North sustainability agenda reacts to decades of car-based expansion (compact city, smart growth); (2) informal settlements: an international agenda on the environmental deficits of informal urbanisation in the Global South; (3) climate change: the overarching concern linking North and South.","It is a two-sided story: urbanists turned to the environment, and environmentalists turned to the city (e.g. The Nature Conservancy's urban programme). Research agenda: historical, multi-spatial, political and representational analysis of urban sustainability thinking, rather than accepting it at face value."],
  concepts:[["City as problem → solution","The discursive shift in global urban-environmental policy."],["Three roots","Sprawl, informal settlements, climate change."],["Methodological cityism","Treating the city as the self-evident scale of analysis and action (Angelo & Wachsmuth 2015)."]],
  quotes:["\"Variations in urban form are systematically linked to socio-environmental outcomes\" (as quoted on the slides)"]},
 fig:`<svg viewBox="0 0 620 190" role="img" aria-label="Three roots of the city as sustainability solution"><g font-size="12">${[["Sprawl","Global North · car-based expansion",20],["Informal settlements","Global South · UN-Habitat 1978",220],["Climate change","links North and South",420]].map(([t,s,x])=>`<rect x="${x}" y="20" width="180" height="60" rx="8" class="bxi"/><text x="${x+90}" y="45" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="700">${t}</text><text x="${x+90}" y="64" text-anchor="middle" class="mut" font-size="11">${s}</text><path d="M${x+90} 80 L310 125" class="ln" stroke-width="1.5"/>`).join('')}<rect x="190" y="125" width="240" height="46" rx="8" class="bxg"/><text x="310" y="146" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="700">'Cities can save the planet'</text><text x="310" y="163" text-anchor="middle" class="mut" font-size="11">1970s roots → 1990s intensification</text></g></svg>`,cap:"Angelo & Wachsmuth's explanation of the shift.",
 rec:[{cite:"McPhearson et al. (2021). Radical changes are needed for transformations to a good Anthropocene. npj Urban Sustainability 1.",doi:"10.1038/s42949-021-00017-x",basis:B_ABS,abs:"The scale and pace of human activity demand radical departures from the status quo to stay within planetary boundaries. Society's steering arms (finance, law, politics, governance) must be realigned, recognising the connection of social, ecological and technological systems. Five principles: rethinking growth, efficiency, the state, the commons, and justice.",slides:"L2: 'Relation between urban growth and sustainability problems' figure; the 'dystopian Anthropocene' quote (status quo, inequality, growth-focused, law cementing the status quo); 'radical planning shifts are needed', coupling social, ecological and technological systems. L3: the five principles as the 'normative approach'."},
 {cite:"Krähmer, K. (2020). Are green cities sustainable? A degrowth critique of sustainable urban development in Copenhagen. European Planning Studies 29(7).",basis:B_ABS,abs:"Copenhagen (European Green Capital 2014) seen through degrowth. Absolute decoupling is infeasible. Three flaws: (1) externalisation: only local emissions count, while consumption-based emissions stay high; (2) efficiency ≠ lower impact, just slower growth of impact; (3) sustainability as a 'green fix' for competitiveness and growth, which raises consumption. This does not mean rejecting all typical measures.",slides:"L2: 'The limits to planning: can the city really be sustainable? (de- or post-growth)'. L3: listed under the critical perspective (with Næss and post-growth)."}],
 exam:"Essay: 'Are cities the cause of or the solution to unsustainability?' Structure: pessimistic vs optimistic evidence (slides), then why the solution-framing emerged (Angelo & Wachsmuth), then its limits (Krähmer: externalisation, efficiency, green fix; measurement problems)."},

{id:"p3",n:3,theme:1,title:"Sustainability: a planning dilemma",who:"Shaun Smith",slides:true,
 lecture:["Three perspectives on planning and urban sustainability: normative (McPhearson's five principles; EEA; UN-Habitat), dilemma-based (Campbell's triangle) and critical (Krähmer, Næss, post-growth).","Planning is 'nearly always dilemmatic' (Khakee 2020): the future is uncertain. Wicked problems (Rittel & Webber) and 'messes' (Ackoff). Type I (tame), Type II (definable, no clear solution) and Type III (neither: continual learning) (DeFries & Nagendra 2017). A dilemmas paradigm: many stakeholders and interpretations, trade-offs at best and conflicts at worst, so conflict resolution is key: a 'viable compromise between sustainable and unsustainable practices' (Campbell 2016). Capitalism 'creates conditions of unceasing growth, upheaval, and disruption'.","Green gentrification as the core dilemma: greening raises land values and displaces those it was meant to serve (Gould & Lewis 2017: a 'green growth machine'; Anguelovski 2015: green LULUs; Checker 2011). Barcelona: 18 parks, where gentrification depended on location (old town yes, isolated periphery no; Anguelovski et al. 2018). Atlanta BeltLine: home values rose 17.9–26.6% more within half a mile (Immergluck & Balan 2018). Anguelovski et al. 2022: greening is the lead explainer of gentrification in 8 of 17 cities, integrated in 6, subsidiary in 3. Responses: 'just green enough' (Wolch et al. 2014), anti-displacement planning, community land trusts."],
 man:{cite:"Campbell, S.D. (1996). Green cities, growing cities, just cities? Urban planning and the contradictions of sustainable development. JAPA 62(3), 296–312.",status:"full",
  one:"Planners work within the tension among three aims (economic growth, environmental protection, social equity): the planner's triangle. Sustainable development sits at its unreachable centre and can only be approached indirectly, by confronting and resolving the conflicts.",
  summary:["Nothing in planning steers it towards green, growth or justice. Its conflicts go to 'the historic core of planning' and are not just misunderstandings between disciplines, but real clashes of interest.","Three conflicts: property conflict (economy ↔ equity: private sector both resists and needs intervention; property's contradictory nature); resource conflict (economy ↔ environment: business resists regulation of nature but needs it to conserve resources, e.g. timber vs spotted owl); development conflict (equity ↔ environment: the most elusive, e.g. protecting nature vs poverty reduction, elite environmentalism; Krumholz's rail vs bus choice in Cleveland).","Sustainable development as currently formulated romanticises a sustainable past and is 'too vaguely holistic'. Planners need social theory plus environmental thinking, and substantive skills plus conflict-resolution techniques, to confront economic and environmental injustice. Nature is socially constructed, not 'man vs nature'."],
  concepts:[["Planner's triangle","Green (environment) · growing (economy) · just (equity), with sustainability at the centre."],["Property conflict","Economic growth ↔ social equity."],["Resource conflict","Economic growth ↔ environmental protection."],["Development conflict","Social equity ↔ environmental protection: the most elusive."],["Three planner types","Economic development planner, environmental planner, equity planner."]],
  quotes:["\"This center cannot be reached directly, but only approximately and indirectly, through a sustained period of confronting and resolving the triangle's conflicts.\""]},
 fig:`<svg viewBox="0 0 620 300" role="img" aria-label="Campbell's planner's triangle"><g font-size="12"><polygon points="310,30 90,260 530,260" class="bx" stroke-width="2"/>
 <circle cx="310" cy="30" r="8" class="fg"/><circle cx="90" cy="260" r="8" class="fi"/><circle cx="530" cy="260" r="8" class="acc"/>
 <text x="310" y="18" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="700">Green · environmental protection</text><text x="90" y="285" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="700">Growing · economic growth</text><text x="530" y="285" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="700">Just · social equity</text>
 <text x="180" y="140" text-anchor="end" class="acc" font-weight="600">resource conflict</text><text x="440" y="140" class="acc" font-weight="600">development conflict</text><text x="310" y="252" text-anchor="middle" class="acc" font-weight="600">property conflict</text>
 <circle cx="310" cy="185" r="26" class="bxg"/><text x="310" y="182" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10">sustainable</text><text x="310" y="195" text-anchor="middle" font-size="10">development</text></g></svg>`,cap:"Campbell (1996): the centre is reached only indirectly, through the conflicts.",
 rec:[{cite:"Campbell, S.D. (2016). The Planner's Triangle Revisited: Sustainability and the Evolution of a Planning Ideal That Can't Stand Still. JAPA 82(4).",basis:B_KNOW,abs:"A 20-year reflection on the triangle: sustainability has become mainstream but remains a moving target ('can't stand still'). New pressures such as climate change, resilience and the gap between rhetoric and implementation reshape the conflicts; planners should treat sustainability as an evolving ideal worked out through conflict, not a fixed end-state.",slides:"L3: quoted for the dilemmas paradigm: the planner seeks a 'viable compromise between sustainable and unsustainable practices' (Campbell 2016); the role is not to find 'perfect' solutions."}],
 exam:"Almost certain essay material: use the triangle to analyse a case (e.g. green gentrification = development conflict; Barcelona, BeltLine). Show you know all three conflicts and why the centre is unreachable."},

{id:"p4",n:4,theme:2,title:"The Dutch planning system",who:"Patrick Witte",slides:true,
 lecture:["Why a planning system? To balance tensions: flexibility vs legal certainty, property rights vs public regulation, expertise vs participation, central vs decentral, comprehensive vs sectoral. Functions: spatial quality, arranging the spatial effects of sectors, comprehensive weighing of claims, coordination, democratic justification, legal protection and certainty. Planning is part of the executive (trias politica). Art. 21 of the Constitution: habitability and protection of the living environment.",
 "History: a Napoleonic tradition (top-down) yet a decentralised unitary state (Thorbecke 1848; 'House of Thorbecke' vertical coordination). National = strategic (>10 yrs), provinces = tactical (5–10), municipalities = operational (<5; binding zoning). Pendulum swings: beyond blueprint and the 'makeable society', beyond entrepreneurial and participatory planning, yet none of these has gone.",
 "Three municipal strategies: inside (public land instruments), outside (acting as a private stakeholder: active land policy) and alongside (open planning, 'polderen': early stakeholder involvement, draagvlak, extra-legal covenants; informal first, law codifies later). Active land policy: buy, service and sell land (VINEX → NOVEX), a 'double hat' problem, only works in rising markets (municipal losses 2008–10; Rijnenburg again). Omgevingswet: care for the living environment, the Omgevingsvisie and Omgevingsplan, complementary task allocation (stronger provinces, project decisions). Instruments: pre-emption right (voorkeursrecht), expropriation, leasehold (erfpacht).",
 "Just city? Informal planning can mean co-creation or tokenism; active land policy means control vs financial risk; public instruments mean legitimacy vs rigidity. Wicked problems are beyond state or market alone. Governance raises questions: who owns the land, who is represented, the meta-governance problem, grey accountability, power imbalances."],
 man:{cite:"Witte, Wiegmans & Louw (2025). More claims than land: multi-facetted land use challenges in the port-city interface. Journal of Transport Geography 124.",status:"full",
  one:"In dense port-cities (Rotterdam, Amsterdam) the net port area is stable, but land-use claims from energy transition, circular economy, climate adaptation, housing, biodiversity and regulation are multiplying: cumulatively there are 'more claims than land', so conflicts will intensify.",
  summary:["The port-city is usually analysed one-dimensionally (economic efficiency) and at one level. The authors argue for a multi-facetted, multi-level view (port region, port-city interface, waterfront).","Method: quantitative analysis of land supply over time in Rotterdam and Amsterdam plus a qualitative analysis of port visions and annual reports. Available land has declined over decades, with many internal dynamics. Rotterdam's coastal extension gives more 'puzzling space' than confined Amsterdam (e.g. Haven-Stad housing pressure).","Mitigation framework: distinguish levels (region–port-city–waterfront) and types of claims (urban, environmental, economic) to structure conflict types and solutions across institutional contexts."],
  concepts:[["Port-city interface","Zone where port functions and urban functions meet and compete."],["More claims than land","Cumulative claims exceed available land even if net claims don't."],["Multi-level port-city","Port region · port-city interface · waterfront."],["Puzzling space","Room to rearrange land uses (larger in Rotterdam than Amsterdam)."]],quotes:[]},
 fig:`<svg viewBox="0 0 620 200" role="img" aria-label="Municipal strategies inside, outside, alongside"><g font-size="12"><rect x="210" y="40" width="200" height="120" rx="10" class="bxi"/><text x="310" y="95" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="700">Formal planning system</text><text x="310" y="113" text-anchor="middle" class="mut" font-size="11">Omgevingswet · Omgevingsplan</text>
 <text x="310" y="140" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11">INSIDE: pre-emption, expropriation</text>
 <rect x="20" y="70" width="160" height="60" rx="8" class="bxw"/><text x="100" y="95" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="700">OUTSIDE</text><text x="100" y="113" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11">active land policy</text>
 <rect x="440" y="70" width="160" height="60" rx="8" class="bxg"/><text x="520" y="95" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="700">ALONGSIDE</text><text x="520" y="113" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11">open planning, 'polderen'</text>
 <text x="310" y="190" text-anchor="middle" class="mut" font-size="11">National: strategic · Province: tactical · Municipality: operational (House of Thorbecke)</text></g></svg>`,cap:"Witte's three municipal intervention strategies.",
 rec:[],exam:"Comparison material for theme 2: a strong formal system and polder culture vs Chinese NTSP and African cities. Know the three strategies, the double-hat problem and the Omgevingswet basics."},

{id:"p5",n:5,theme:2,title:"Fight, flight or freeze? Governing Moerdijk",who:"Patrick Witte",slides:true,
 lecture:["Moerdijk: a village next to a major port. 2011 Chemie-Pack chemical fire; 2013 port development strategy ('Port Vision 2030' → compensation planning, the 'Moerdijk regulation'; Committee Nijpels investments of €10m + €8m); 2025 renewed debate on the village's future (PowerPort, energy transition).","Spatial externalities of port regionalisation: villages vs 'the greater good'. Three governance approaches: Fight (village stays: Pernis, Rozenburg, Heijplaat; Doel?, Ruigoord?), Freeze (village slowly disappears: Nieuwesluis, Blankenburg; Lillo, Oorderen…; Lützerath), Flight (village moves: Kiruna, Weiwerd, Oterdum, Heveskes, Immerath).","Discussion: why is Moerdijk strategic? NIMBY or more at stake? Who takes the initiative and decides? What is a just outcome: stay, move, compensation? The same mandatory reading as lecture 4 applies (more claims than land)."],
 man:{cite:"Same mandatory reading as lecture 4: Witte et al. (2025).",status:"full",one:"Applies 'more claims than land' to a village-scale conflict between port growth, the energy transition and residents.",summary:[],concepts:[["Fight / flight / freeze","Village stays / relocates / is left to decline."],["Compensation planning","Mitigating harm to residents instead of preventing it."]],quotes:[]},
 fig:"",cap:"",rec:[],exam:"A good case for essays combining Campbell's triangle (economy vs equity vs environment) with justice (who bears the costs of the 'greater good')."},

{id:"p6",n:6,theme:2,title:"Planning for sustainability in African cities",who:"Shaun Smith",slides:true,
 lecture:["Over half of Africa's urban population lives in informal settlements, often on marginal, risky land. Colonial planning (British: military planning, then master planning via Town Planning Ordinances 1920s–50s) meant evictions, commodification of land, dual land laws, institutionalised racism, 'public health' discourses and green belts as exclusion (Njoh: domination, intimidation, surveillance). Legacies: fragmentation (Maputo: services only for the colonial city), elite capture of European areas, costly services, assumptions of tax bases and data capacity that did not hold.",
 "Postcolonial thinking: colonial knowledge production served domination; theory shouldn't be produced mainly in the West (Miraftab: planning treats Southern cities that don't fit Western models as failures). Post-independence master planning persisted (Accra; Dodoma under Ujamaa), centralised, by foreign consultants, little participation, often elite-captured. Southern urbanism: 'seeing from the South' (Watson), 'ordinary cities' (Robinson), conflicting rationalities.",
 "Slums and informality: Roy (2007/2011): informality as a mode of urbanisation and a state practice of (de)legitimising (wealthy malls can be 'informal' yet legitimate). Upgrading shifted from resettlement to in-situ, participatory upgrading (risk: benefits landlords). De Soto: give titles to 'heroic entrepreneurs' to unlock capital. Critics: tenure security doesn't need title; titling is costly; it favours those who can exploit markets.",
 "Trends: insurgent planning (Miraftab: invited vs invented spaces), inverse governmentality, co-production; TOD and 'Corridors of Freedom' (Johannesburg), only partly realised; gated communities and new cities (Tatu City, Kenya) cause fragmentation, evictions and prices out of touch with context (Keeton & Nijhuis 2019)."],
 man:{cite:"De Satgé, R. & Watson, V. (2018). Urban Planning in the Global South: Conflicting Rationalities in Contested Urban Space. Palgrave, ch. 1–2.",status:"full",
  one:"Planning theory and 'best practices' are place-blind, built on Global North assumptions (strong civil society, capable institutions, formal economies). In Southern cities, state–society engagement is shaped less by a shared 'public interest' than by deep conflicting rationalities between techno-managerial, marketised state planning and marginalised communities surviving under informality.",
  summary:["Ch. 1: a southern turn challenges Northern knowledge assumed to hold everywhere. The Global South differs (rapid urbanisation, weak civil society, under-resourced institutions, informal economies, poverty) and is shaped by colonial histories; 'southern' is a perspective, not a geography. Imported 'best practices' (Garden City, neighbourhood unit, Radburn) justified apartheid removals. Case: Langa, Cape Town (1923 Garden City township), the N2 Gateway megaproject (Breaking New Ground policy, 2004) that sought to 'eradicate' informality at Joe Slovo settlement.",
  "Watson (2003) coined conflicting rationalities from a Cape Town inquiry: clashing state and shack-dweller worldviews that Habermasian consensus planning cannot resolve. Planners must work with deep difference. Method: a paradigmatic single case (Flyvbjerg), retroductive, with visual methods (photo-elicitation) and interviews in English and Xhosa.",
  "Ch. 2: a 'southern theorising project' rather than a new North/South binary: critical perspectives on existing theory, rooted in place ('origin narratives', phronesis), with meso-level generalisation. Rationality = a way of seeing, linked to power (Foucault; Flyvbjerg & Richardson): power defines what counts as rational; 'technical' framing is an exercise of power. Identity (race, class, gender, language) shapes rationality; language filters meaning. Governmentality, the shadow state. The 'interface' (Li 2007) is where rationalities clash. Proposition: conflict of rationalities, not a public interest, is the starting point."],
  concepts:[["Southern turn / southern theorising project","Critique of place-blind Northern theory; a perspective, not a geography."],["Conflicting rationalities","Techno-managerial, modernising, marketised state planning vs survival logics of marginalised, often informal residents."],["Interface","Times and places where rationalities meet and contest (Li 2007)."],["Rationality as power","Power defines what counts as rational/reality (Foucault, Flyvbjerg)."],["Best-practice 'landing'","Imported models applied without checking fit."],["Informality (Roy)","A mode of urbanisation; the state decides what is legitimate."]],
  quotes:["\"categories such as state, civil society, property ownership… are assumed to be universal, but can have severe consequences for those unable or unwilling to conform\" (de Satgé & Watson 2018, on the slides)"]},
 fig:`<svg viewBox="0 0 620 190" role="img" aria-label="Conflicting rationalities at the interface"><g font-size="12"><rect x="20" y="40" width="220" height="100" rx="10" class="bxi"/><text x="130" y="66" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="700">State + market</text><text x="130" y="86" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11">techno-managerial</text><text x="130" y="102" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11">modernising · marketised</text><text x="130" y="118" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11">'eradicate informality'</text>
 <rect x="380" y="40" width="220" height="100" rx="10" class="bxw"/><text x="490" y="66" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="700">Marginalised residents</text><text x="490" y="86" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11">survival · informality</text><text x="490" y="102" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11">kinship networks</text><text x="490" y="118" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11">'illegality'</text>
 <rect x="275" y="55" width="70" height="70" rx="8" class="bxa"/><text x="310" y="94" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="700" font-size="11">interface</text>
 <text x="310" y="175" text-anchor="middle" class="mut" font-size="11">Not a shared 'public interest' → consensus is not the starting point (vs Habermas)</text></g></svg>`,cap:"De Satgé & Watson's core proposition.",
 rec:[{cite:"Watson, V. (2014). Learning planning from the south: ideas from the new urban frontiers. In Parnell & Oldfield (eds), Routledge Handbook on Cities of the Global South.",basis:B_KNOW,abs:"Argues that planning theory should learn from Southern contexts ('new urban frontiers' of rapid, informal urbanisation) rather than export Northern models: take informality, weak states and conflicting rationalities as starting points, and value situated, co-produced knowledge.",slides:"Not named on the slides. Related: 'Seeing from the South' (Watson 2008/2009) and the conflicting-rationalities slides."},
 {cite:"Castán Broto et al. (2022). Co-production outcomes for urban equality. Current Research in Environmental Sustainability 4.",basis:B_ABS,abs:"Co-production (citizens and institutions delivering a shared outcome) answers critiques of participatory planning and challenges epistemic injustice. Across six cities it can build long-term, adaptable partnerships for urban equality: distribution, reciprocal recognition, political access and plural knowledges. It needs constant adjustment and trade-offs, and its transformative impact isn't always measurable.",slides:"Not named on the slides. Related: 'community based informal processes', insurgent planning, invited vs invented spaces."}],
 exam:"Theme-2 essay core: 'Can planning theories from one context be transferred to another?' (the literal portfolio question). Use conflicting rationalities, colonial legacies, Roy on informality, and contrast with the Dutch polder model and China."},

{id:"p7",n:7,theme:2,title:"Planning for sustainability in Chinese cities",who:"Yanliu Lin",slides:true,
 lecture:["Socialist planning (1950s–70s): strong state, no market, work units, 156 Soviet-aided industrial projects, satellite towns (Shanghai 1958) that failed to attract people. Legacies: hukou (1958) rural/urban registration that restricts migrants' access to services, and the dual land system (state-owned urban land with transferable use rights vs rural collective land that must be expropriated before urban use).","1989 City Planning Act: 'one report, two permits'; planning serves growth and controls urban, not rural, land, which led to sprawl. Shenzhen: from villages to a metropolis; 2,042 urban villages house ~60% of residents. Fragmentation before 2019: city, land-use, regional, ecological, transport and main-function-zoning plans each had their own maps. 1994 fiscal reform (locals keep ~25% of tax) → land finance → a land-driven growth coalition (cheap industrial land, expensive residential land), with environmental and social costs.","Collaborative turn? Legalised participation (2006, 2008 Act) became 'authoritarian deliberation': Enning Road, Guangzhou went from demolition and protest (2007) to micro-regeneration and a co-creation committee (2018). This reduced conflict but had limited influence, came late, and involved unequal power. It is instrumental rather than normative.","Experiments: eco-cities (>259; Sino-Singapore Tianjin Eco-City), greenways (Guangdong, 20,000 km, often reduced to transport), smart cities (>900; scale over effectiveness, hardware over software). Do these top-down experiments stay isolated showcases? Territorial Spatial Planning (2018–19): one system, one ministry (MNR), five levels, three types, three control lines (ecological red line, permanent basic farmland, urban development boundary), 'one map', a new law. Guangzhou 2021–35: 15-minute living circles, ecological corridors; weak legal backing until 2025."],
 man:{cite:"Liu, Y. & Zhou, Y. (2021). Territory spatial planning and national governance system in China. Land Use Policy 102, 105288.",status:"full",
  one:"China's spatial planning was fragmented (many plan types, overlapping and conflicting, complex approvals, frequent revisions, weak implementation, no superior law), which caused imbalanced development, ecological damage and resource pressure. The national territory spatial planning (NTSP) system and legislation integrate these into one system, but major challenges remain.",
  summary:["A review of the evolution of Chinese spatial planning against strategic spatial planning in Europe (ESDP 1999). Problems: types, overlaps, approvals, revisions, implementation.","Measures: treat territorial space as a system; move from sectoral/special plans to integrated NTSP (the 2018 institutional reform, MNR); push for NTSP legislation as a milestone for governing space by law.","Challenges: formulation and implementation must fit the times, modernisation laws and local conditions; the authors give countermeasures (legal basis, coordination across levels, the 'one map' data platform, balancing development and protection)."],
  concepts:[["NTSP","National territory spatial planning: one integrated system."],["Three control lines","Ecological protection red line · permanent basic farmland · urban development boundary."],["'Multiple plans in one'","Integrating land-use, urban-rural, main-function zoning and other plans."],["Land finance","Local revenue from land sales after the 1994 fiscal reform."],["Authoritarian deliberation","State-bounded participation with limited empowerment."]],quotes:[]},
 fig:`<svg viewBox="0 0 620 200" role="img" aria-label="From fragmented plans to territorial spatial planning"><g font-size="11">${["City plan","Land-use plan","Regional plan","Eco protection","Transport","Function zoning"].map((t,i)=>`<rect x="20" y="${14+i*30}" width="150" height="24" rx="6" class="bx"/><text x="95" y="${30+i*30}" text-anchor="middle">${t}</text><path d="M170 ${26+i*30} L260 100" class="ln"/>`).join('')}
 <rect x="260" y="60" width="160" height="80" rx="10" class="bxg"/><text x="340" y="88" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="700" font-size="12">Territorial Spatial</text><text x="340" y="104" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="700" font-size="12">Planning (2019)</text><text x="340" y="124" text-anchor="middle" class="mut">one map · one ministry</text>
 <text x="440" y="40" font-weight="700" font-size="12">3 control lines</text><text x="440" y="60">● ecological red line</text><text x="440" y="78">● permanent farmland</text><text x="440" y="96">● urban development boundary</text><text x="440" y="130" font-weight="700" font-size="12">5 levels · 3 plan types</text><text x="440" y="150" class="mut">national → township</text></g></svg>`,cap:"The integration reform discussed in lecture 7 and Liu & Zhou.",
 rec:[{cite:"Fu, Y. & Zhang, X. (2017). Planning for sustainable cities? Master plans of eco, low-carbon and conventional new towns in China. Habitat International 63.",basis:B_ABS,abs:"A content analysis comparing master plans of eco, low-carbon and conventional new towns. Eco and low-carbon plans do focus more on sustainability, differently: eco towns target a sustainable way of life (evenly spread, stress on an aesthetically pleasing, liveable environment); low-carbon towns target sustainable production (industrial integration and transformation). Plans show intentions only; it's too early to call them failures.",slides:"Not named on the slides. Related: the eco-city slides (>259 cities; eco-state + local entrepreneurialism + policy mobility; Tianjin) and the question whether top-down experiments stay isolated showcases."}],
 exam:"Know hukou, the dual land system, land finance, fragmentation vs NTSP, authoritarian deliberation. Essay: 'Can top-down sustainability experiments transform planning?' or a comparison of China with the Netherlands and Africa."},

{id:"p8",n:8,theme:2,title:"Urban sustainability transitions",who:"Shaun Smith",slides:false,
 lecture:["Slides not uploaded yet (lecture on 1 Oct). This page is built from the mandatory reading and the recommended abstracts."],
 man:{cite:"Hodson, M., Geels, F.W. & McMeekin, A. (2017). Reconfiguring Urban Sustainability Transitions, Analysing Multiplicity. Sustainability 9(2), 299.",status:"full",
  one:"Urban transitions aren't about single technological or social innovations but about how multiple innovations are experimented with, combined and reconfigured in existing urban contexts, and how that is governed. Innovation lies in the form of reconfiguration.",
  summary:["Many plans exist, but sustainable infrastructure on the ground is uneven. Using urban sustainable mobility, the paper bridges socio-technical transitions (national systems, MLP) and urban geography (space, place).","Three foundational themes: (1) experiments reconfigure relationships between multiple systems (automobility, rail, bus, cycling), linking experimentation with systems and spaces; (2) multi-level urban governance: arrangements may compete, co-exist or be coordinated; (3) competing conceptions of sustainability set different goals (e.g. mobility: reduce the need to travel, modal shift, shorter trips).","A preliminary framework to embrace multiplicity through 'reconfiguration'. It calls for theoretically informed ethnography and comparative work."],
  concepts:[["Reconfiguration","Recombining multiple innovations and systems in a specific city."],["Multiplicity","Many possible innovations, governance arrangements and sustainability conceptions."],["Socio-technical transition","Shift of a whole system (technology, practices, institutions); MLP: niche–regime–landscape."],["Experimentation–systems–spaces","Theme 1 of Hodson et al."]],quotes:[]},
 fig:"",cap:"",
 rec:[{cite:"Monstadt et al. (2022). Rethinking the governance of urban infrastructural transformations. COSUST 55.",basis:B_ABS,abs:"An 'infrastructural turn': infrastructures are no longer black-boxed in sustainability transformations. The paper synthesises emerging governance approaches: appraising alternative pathways via 'futuring', enacting them via experimentation, supported by cross-domain coordination and new assessment methods, which also surface tensions and contradictions.",slides:"No slides yet."},
 {cite:"Hölscher & Frantzeskaki (2021). Perspectives on urban transformation research: transformations in, of, and by cities. Urban Transformations 3.",basis:B_ABS,abs:"Three perspectives: transformations IN cities (places where local–global factors drive or constrain change), OF cities (systemic change in infrastructure, culture, practice), BY cities (cities' regional and global impacts; cities as agents of change). Each clarifies different questions and has limits; research should bridge them.",slides:"No slides yet."},
 {cite:"Frantzeskaki, Castán Broto, Coenen & Loorbach (2017). Urban sustainability transitions (book intro), pp. 1–19.",basis:B_KNOW,abs:"Introduces urban sustainability transitions: cities as sites where transitions are experimented with and contested; brings transition studies (niches, regimes, transition management) into dialogue with urban studies, emphasising place, agency, politics and multi-actor governance.",slides:"No slides yet."}],
 exam:"Add the lecture slides after 1 Oct. Likely link: transitions as reconfiguration vs Campbell's conflicts; experiments (L9) as the tool."},

{id:"p9",n:9,theme:3,title:"Urban experimentation and co-creation",who:"Niki Frantzeskaki",slides:false,
 lecture:["Slides not uploaded yet (5 Oct)."],
 man:{cite:"Bulkeley, H. et al. (2019). Urban living laboratories: conducting the experimental city? European Urban and Regional Studies 26(4), 317–335.",status:"full",
  one:"Experimentation is not one thing. Comparing 40 European urban living labs, the authors find three ideal types (strategic, civic, organic) placed along four laboratory dispositions (trial, enclave, demonstration, platform).",
  summary:["The 'experimental city' is an arena through which urban sustainability is governed: demonstration projects, transition management, grassroots innovation.","Strategic ULLs: led by national or regional agencies and large partners, sponsored testing of new technologies or first-mover advantage (e.g. Aspern Urban Lakeside, Vienna). Civic ULLs: municipality-led, aiming at urban improvement and legitimacy. Organic ULLs: grassroots and community-driven, social and environmental wellbeing (e.g. Stapeln, Malmö).","Dispositions: trial (controlled test in 'real-world' conditions), enclave (a bounded microcosm set apart), demonstration (showing what's possible to others), platform (an open space for continuing interaction and new experiments). ULLs matter for how, where and by whom urban futures are shaped."],
  concepts:[["Urban living lab (ULL)","A site-based, co-creative experiment in real-life urban settings."],["Strategic / civic / organic ULLs","National/corporate-led · municipality-led · community-led."],["Trial / enclave / demonstration / platform","Four laboratory dispositions."]],quotes:[]},
 fig:`<svg viewBox="0 0 620 190" role="img" aria-label="ULL types and dispositions"><g font-size="12"><text x="20" y="22" font-weight="700">Types</text>${[["Strategic","national / corporate",20],["Civic","municipality",220],["Organic","community",420]].map(([t,s,x])=>`<rect x="${x}" y="32" width="180" height="46" rx="8" class="bxi"/><text x="${x+90}" y="52" text-anchor="middle" font-weight="700">${t}</text><text x="${x+90}" y="68" text-anchor="middle" class="mut" font-size="11">${s}</text>`).join('')}
 <text x="20" y="112" font-weight="700">Dispositions</text>${["trial","enclave","demonstration","platform"].map((t,i)=>`<rect x="${20+i*150}" y="122" width="130" height="36" rx="18" class="bxg"/><text x="${85+i*150}" y="145" text-anchor="middle">${t}</text>`).join('')}<text x="20" y="182" class="mut" font-size="11">from control (trial, enclave) → openness (platform)</text></g></svg>`,cap:"Bulkeley et al. (2019) typology.",
 rec:[{cite:"Frantzeskaki et al. (2025). Premises, practices and politics of co-creation for urban sustainability transitions. Urban Transformations 7.",basis:B_ABS,abs:"Why co-create? It bridges and weaves knowledge for place-based transitions, emancipates urban policy and planning, and advances transdisciplinary research (vs mere participation). How? Advocacy, communication, leadership and organisation, plus often-overlooked creativity, playfulness, emotional intelligence, receptivity and collaborative learning. With whom? The politics of inclusion (avoiding tokenism). Measure impact against its premises with reflexive metrics.",slides:"No slides yet."},
 {cite:"Raven, von Wirth, Bai et al. (2026). The future of urban experimentation through ten critical lessons from decades of practice. Nature Cities 3.",basis:B_PART,abs:"Based on eight databases covering almost 2,000 urban experiments since Kyoto, Paris, the SDGs and the New Urban Agenda: ten lessons across key processes, politics and governance, and impact. A call to embed experimentation as an enduring governance practice, develop whole-of-systems approaches across sectors and silos, and build relational and institutional infrastructures for reflexive monitoring and evaluation. (The ten lessons themselves are behind the paywall.)",slides:"No slides yet."}],
 exam:"Watch the 5 Oct slides. Expected link: experimentation as the planner's tool for transitions (L8), and its politics (who is included) back to justice (L3, L6)."},

{id:"p10",n:10,theme:3,title:"Digital planning for sustainable urban futures",who:"Yanliu Lin",slides:false,lecture:["Slides not uploaded yet (8 Oct)."],
 man:{cite:"Lin, Y.L., Geertman, S., Witte, P. & Pinto, N. (2025). Editorial: Digital planning for sustainable urban future. CEUS 122, 102334.",status:"full",
  one:"Digital planning (PSS, urban analytics, digital twins, AI, VR/AR, gamification, e-participation) promises more efficient, effective and inclusive planning, but brings implementation, usability, bias, digital-divide and cybersecurity problems. It requires new ways of planning, not just new tools.",
  summary:["Cities face climate change, ageing, polarisation, unaffordability and mobility issues, and planning struggles with complexity and conflicting interests. Many countries are digitising planning (national plan registers; the UK Digital Taskforce; the Dutch Omgevingswet digitalising participation).","Opportunities: engagement via online platforms, crowdsourcing and social-media analysis; real-time and big data; digital twins for scenarios and consensus-building.","Risks: mismatch between technology supply and demand, user-unfriendly tools, socio-political bias, exclusion, a skills gap in authorities and universities, uneven and fragmented integration. Digitalisation needs proper methods and communication strategies, with insights from academia."],
  concepts:[["Digital planning","Digital technologies and data-driven approaches to improve the efficiency, effectiveness and inclusivity of planning."],["PSS / PSScience","Planning support systems / the science behind them (Geertman)."],["Digital twin","A virtual replica of the city for simulation and scenarios."],["Digital divide","Unequal access and skills, risking exclusion."]],quotes:[]},fig:"",cap:"",
 rec:[{cite:"Geertman & Witte (2024). From PSScience to digital planning. CEUS 114, 102183.",basis:B_ABS,abs:"There is no accepted definition of digital planning: 'the application of digital technologies and data-driven approaches to enhance efficiency, effectiveness, and inclusivity in planning processes to improve social, economic, and environmental outcomes'. PSScience is the scientific base of practice-oriented digital planning. It needs a quadruple-helix collaboration (government, market, society, education and research).",slides:"No slides yet."}],
 exam:"Link to BPT knowledge debates: digital tools can reinforce a technocratic, positivist view (L11: AI) or support inclusive co-production."},

{id:"p11",n:11,theme:3,title:"AI, planning and sustainability",who:"Yanliu Lin",slides:false,lecture:["Slides not uploaded yet (12 Oct)."],
 man:{cite:"Li, X.Y. & Dang, A.R. (2025). Comment: the disruptive effect of AI on urban planning. Nature Cities 2(7), 568–570.",status:"full",
  one:"Unlike earlier tools (CAD, GIS) that kept humans interpreting, 'urban AI' automates critical-thinking tasks. This threatens planners' judgement, equity, democratic engagement and accountability, and so public trust and professional legitimacy. Harnessing AI responsibly needs critical engagement with its ethics.",
  summary:["Examples: Singapore AI traffic signals, Copenhagen AI energy forecasting, Los Angeles ML for housing allocation.","Four risks: (1) deskilling: algorithmic 'optimal' outputs gain authority and displace practitioner wisdom and value reconciliation; (2) algorithmic bias: data reflect historic inequities (redlining) and ignore unquantifiable values; (3) diminishing democratic engagement: fast automated decisions bypass deliberation; (4) blurred accountability: who is responsible when an algorithmic site choice triggers gentrification?","Needed: transparency, open data, clear accountability, planners keeping normative judgement, and planning education that updates core values."],
  concepts:[["Urban AI","Machine learning that actively shapes governance and physical planning."],["Deskilling","Loss of practitioner judgement through reliance on algorithms."],["Algorithmic bias","Reproducing historic inequities through training data."],["Accountability gap","Unclear responsibility for AI-driven decisions."]],quotes:[]},fig:"",cap:"",
 rec:[{cite:"Yigitcanlar et al. (2020). Contributions and risks of AI in building smarter cities. Energies 13(6), 1473.",basis:B_ABS,abs:"A systematic review of 93 articles across economy, society, environment and governance. AI in smart cities is an emerging field focused on technologies and applications (efficiency, analytics, education, energy, environment, health, land use, security, transport, management). Research on the risks of wider AI use and on coming disruptions is limited.",slides:"No slides yet."}],
 exam:"Strong essay link: AI as a return of rational-comprehensive, expert-led planning (BPT week 3) vs communicative values."},

{id:"p12",n:12,theme:3,title:"Planning for post-growth cities",who:"Shaun Smith",slides:false,lecture:["Slides not uploaded yet (15 Oct)."],
 man:{cite:"Durrant, D., Lamker, C. & Rydin, Y. (2023). The Potential of Post-Growth Planning: Re-Tooling the Planning Profession for Moving beyond Growth. Planning Theory & Practice 24(2), 287–295.",status:"full",
  one:"Planning has bound itself to a pro-growth agenda, and 'sustainable development' has been subsumed into it. Since absolute decoupling isn't happening, planners should re-tool existing instruments (for housing and infrastructure) to delink public goals from growth, and voice uncomfortable truths.",
  summary:["Planning's instruments were built to cope with and foster growth, and success is measured in money. Sustainability became eco-modernist green growth ('almost always possible to label… more development as sustainable', Krähmer). Degrowth is structural and economic; post-growth is a pragmatic, interdisciplinary search for radical alternatives within existing structures.","Decoupling: relative (impact grows slower) vs absolute (impact falls). There is little evidence of absolute decoupling: rebound (Jevons paradox), problem-shifting, overestimated recycling and circularity, underestimated service-economy impacts.","Post-growth tools: exclude land value capture (inherently pro-growth); protect land for public, social and community housing; set targets for self-build; mandatory life-cycle and carbon accounting; cumulative impacts; minimum AND maximum space standards; ceilings (not only targets) on development, redistributed from high-price to high-need areas; plan for shrinkage; reuse the existing stock; restrict second homes and short-term lets. Infrastructure: plan phase-outs alongside new build (cycling at the expense of roads; rail with less air capacity); interrogate 'do nothing' scenarios. Planners move from neutral managers to 'supportive holders of shared leadership', grounded in expertise about consequences."],
  concepts:[["Degrowth vs post-growth","Structural, economic and North–South critique vs pragmatic re-tooling within existing structures."],["Relative vs absolute decoupling","Slower impact growth vs actual impact decline."],["Jevons paradox (rebound)","Efficiency gains increase total use."],["Development ceilings","Absolute limits instead of growth targets."],["Why exclude land value capture?","It depends on creating more monetised development value, which is pro-growth."]],
  quotes:["\"Planners would move towards a role where they are supportive holders of shared leadership rather than neutral managers, process designers, or 'master planners.'\""]},fig:"",cap:"",
 rec:[{cite:"Xue, J. (2021). Urban planning and degrowth: a missing dialogue. Local Environment 27(4).",basis:B_KNOW,abs:"Argues that degrowth scholarship and urban planning have barely engaged with each other: degrowth lacks a spatial and planning dimension, and planning is locked into growth. Xue sketches what degrowth planning could mean (housing sufficiency and less floor space per capita, compact but non-growth-driven urban form, the role of the state and public land).",slides:"No slides yet."},
 {cite:"Savini, Ferreira & von Schönfeld (2022). Uncoupling planning and economic growth: towards post-growth urban principles (intro), Post-growth planning.",basis:B_ABS,abs:"Existing planning instruments depend on ceaseless growth, which erodes planners' capacity to address socio-ecological justice. How can planning be emancipated from the growth imperative, geared to well-being, equality and ecological equilibrium? Key areas: housing, transport and mobility, governance, regulation, food and resource provision, planning worldviews and ethics.",slides:"No slides yet."}],
 exam:"Connect with L1 (Daly: growth vs development; sufficiency), L2 (Krähmer) and L3 (Campbell: growth vertex). A likely essay: 'Is sustainable urban planning possible without growth?'"},

{id:"p13",n:13,theme:3,title:"Planning with nature",who:"Niki Frantzeskaki",slides:false,lecture:["Slides not uploaded yet (19 Oct)."],
 man:{cite:"Hansen, R. et al. (2022). Transformative or piecemeal? Changes in green space planning and governance in eleven European cities. European Planning Studies 31(12), 2401–2424.",status:"full",
  one:"A longitudinal study of 11 European cities: green space planning gets more attention and some cities overcome path dependencies (silos) and co-create with business and civil society. But most change is incremental, limited by maintenance, funding, organisational culture and staff capacity, with social justice and cultural diversity poorly recognised.",
  summary:["Rising high-level support for green space and co-governance, alongside social and ecological pressures.","Deliberate transformative change happened at high levels of local policy (new policies, co-governance cultures). Incremental changes (citizen interest, systems thinking, integrated planning, fewer silos) may seed future transformations.","Constraints are structural (resources, staff) and beyond green-space departments. Multi-scale policy frameworks, change management, training on social aspects, exnovation of 'lock-in' practices, and putting social and environmental justice at the heart of planning are needed."],
  concepts:[["Transformative vs piecemeal/incremental","Deep system change vs gradual adjustments."],["Path dependency / lock-in","E.g. siloed departments."],["Exnovation","Deliberately phasing out lock-in practices."],["Co-governance","Planning green space with citizens and business."]],quotes:[]},fig:"",cap:"",
 rec:[{cite:"Frantzeskaki, N. (2019). Seven lessons for planning nature-based solutions in cities. Environmental Science & Policy 93.",basis:B_ABS,abs:"From 15 NBS experiments in 11 European cities: NBS must be aesthetically appealing; they create new green urban commons; experimenting needs trust in local government; co-creation needs diversity and learning from social innovation; NBS need collaborative governance; an inclusive mission narrative integrates them into many agendas; design them to learn and replicate. NBS are placed as 'superior' to grey infrastructure.",slides:"No slides yet."},
 {cite:"Frantzeskaki, Wijsman, Kabisch & McPhearson (2025). Inter- and transdisciplinary knowledge is critical for NBS to contribute to just urban transformations. PNAS 122(29).",basis:B_ABS,abs:"NBS aim to break unsustainable path dependencies, but they are not inherently just or unjust. Six questions: for what, which nature, where, how, when, for whom. Two opportunities: interdisciplinary work for multifunctionality, and transdisciplinary work with stakeholders on design and siting. Justice should guide from the start: a shift from epistemological injustice to epistemological inclusivity.",slides:"No slides yet."},
 {cite:"Grabowski, McPhearson & Pickett (2023). Transforming US urban green infrastructure planning to address equity. Landscape and Urban Planning 229.",basis:B_ABS,abs:"122 plans from 20 US cities: only 13% define equity or justice; 30% acknowledge Native land; over 90% lack inclusive processes; 80% use GI for hazards and multiple benefits but under 10% identify the causes of uneven vulnerability; few address houselessness or gentrification. Needed: clear definitions of equity, engagement with causes of inequality and displacement, and a transformation towards inclusion.",slides:"No slides yet. Directly relevant to the green gentrification slides of L3."}],
 exam:"Bridges L3 (green gentrification) and L8–9 (transitions, co-creation). A likely essay: 'Can nature-based solutions be transformative and just?'"}
];

const PSC_FC=[
["p1","Brundtland (1987) definition and agenda","Development that meets present needs without compromising future generations; political consensus aim; limits of technology and social organisation, not absolute limits; still pro-growth."],
["p1","Weak vs strong sustainability","Weak: capitals substitutable (neo-classical, Solow). Strong: natural capital complementary, not interchangeable; planetary boundaries."],
["p1","Daly: growth vs development","Growth = getting physically bigger (can't last); development = getting qualitatively better (no limits). Sustainable development was left vague on purpose to build consensus."],
["p1","Daly's three rules","Renewables no faster than they regrow; non-renewables no faster than substitutes are invented; waste no faster than it can be absorbed."],
["p1","Swyngedouw (2010) on sustainability","Post-political: impossible not to support; imagines a harmonious nature to restore through managerial fixes; hides the political question of which natures we want; can be captured by incumbents."],
["p1","Efficiency, sufficiency, consistency","Efficiency: tech gains per unit. Sufficiency: 'seeking enough when more is possible' (self-limitation, egalitarian distribution). Consistency: qualitative change in industrial metabolism mimicking natural cycles."],
["p1","Rudolf & Schmidt's conclusion","Efficiency and sufficiency have limited long-run effects; consistency is promising as systemic change."],
["p1","Purvis et al. (2019) on the three pillars","No single origin and no theoretically rigorous description; it emerged gradually from critiques of growth and UN efforts."],
["p1","Vallance et al. (2011): three social sustainabilities","Development (basic needs, justice, equity), bridge (behaviour change for bio-physical goals), maintenance (preserving socio-cultural characteristics)."],
["p1","Næss (2001): central claims","More ambitious policy is needed; per-capita growth of building stock must stop in rich countries; use substantive long-term goals, not only means-ends rationality; alliance-building instead of all-stakeholder consensus."],
["p1","Form vs process (Neuman, on L1 slides)","Sustainable urban development gives 'primacy of process over form', unlike compact-city form thinking."],
["p2","Cities' share of global impact (L2)","~70% of resources/energy, ~70% of CO₂, 75–80% of waste; DMC share from 50% (2010) to 60% (2050)."],
["p2","Angelo & Wachsmuth's core argument","A shift from city-as-problem to city-as-solution, rooted in reactions to sprawl, informal settlements and climate change (1970s roots, 1990s intensification)."],
["p2","Why is measuring urban sustainability hard? (Tanguay et al.)","Overly broad definitions; no standard classification of indicators; data limits (only ~80 cities have consistent CO₂ data)."],
["p2","Pessimistic vs optimistic view of cities","Pessimistic: growth-oriented planning, injustice, implementation gap, dystopian Anthropocene. Optimistic: agglomeration, innovation, C40, mayors, compact cities, cheap emission cuts."],
["p2","MDGs → SDGs shift","Global South focus → universal; more social and ecological emphasis; a standalone urban goal (SDG 11)."],
["p2","McPhearson's 'dystopian Anthropocene'","The status quo continued: inequality, injustice, growth-focused economics, law cementing the status quo, worsening environmental ills."],
["p2","Krähmer's three flaws of green Copenhagen","Externalisation (local emissions only), efficiency ≠ lower impact, green fix for growth and competitiveness."],
["p3","Three perspectives on planning and sustainability (L3)","Normative (McPhearson), dilemma-based (Campbell), critical (Krähmer, Næss, post-growth)."],
["p3","McPhearson's five principles","Rethinking growth, efficiency, the state, the commons, justice."],
["p3","Campbell's planner's triangle","Green (environment), growing (economy), just (equity); sustainable development at the centre, reachable only indirectly through the conflicts."],
["p3","Property conflict","Economy ↔ equity: the private sector resists yet needs intervention (property's contradictory nature)."],
["p3","Resource conflict","Economy ↔ environment: business resists regulation of nature but needs it to conserve resources."],
["p3","Development conflict","Equity ↔ environment: the most elusive (e.g. protecting nature vs reducing poverty; elite environmentalism)."],
["p3","Campbell's critique of sustainable development","It romanticises a sustainable past and is 'too vaguely holistic'; planners need social theory plus conflict resolution."],
["p3","Wicked problem types I–III (DeFries & Nagendra)","I: tame/technical; II: definable but no clear-cut solution; III: neither, needing continual learning."],
["p3","Green gentrification","Greening raises land values and displaces the residents it was meant to serve (Gould & Lewis: green growth machine)."],
["p3","Atlanta BeltLine finding","Home values rose 17.9–26.6% more within half a mile (2011–15) (Immergluck & Balan 2018)."],
["p3","Barcelona parks finding","Green gentrification depends on location and market pressure: old town and industrial districts yes, isolated periphery no."],
["p3","'Just green enough'","Wolch et al. (2014): modest, community-directed greening scaled to existing residents' needs."],
["p4","Three municipal intervention strategies (Witte)","Inside (public land instruments), outside (private stakeholder: active land policy), alongside (open planning, 'polderen')."],
["p4","Dutch government levels and plan horizons","National: strategic (>10 yrs); provinces: tactical (5–10); municipalities: operational (<5, binding zoning)."],
["p4","Active land policy risks","'Double hat' problem; only works in rising markets; municipal losses in 2008–10 (Rijnenburg again)."],
["p4","Omgevingswet key elements","Care for the living environment; Omgevingsvisie (strategic vision) + Omgevingsplan; complementary task allocation; stronger provinces; project decisions."],
["p4","Witte et al. (2025): 'more claims than land'","Net port land is stable, but cumulative claims (energy, circularity, climate, housing, nature) exceed the available land in Rotterdam and Amsterdam."],
["p5","Fight / flight / freeze (Moerdijk)","Village stays (Pernis) / village moves (Kiruna) / village slowly disappears (Lützerath, Doel)."],
["p6","Colonial planning legacies in African cities","Fragmentation (Maputo), dual land laws, evictions, commodified land, racialised 'public health' zoning, elite capture, costly services."],
["p6","Conflicting rationalities (Watson 2003; de Satgé & Watson 2018)","A clash between techno-managerial, marketised state planning and marginalised communities' survival logics, so consensus/public interest is not the starting point."],
["p6","The 'interface' (de Satgé & Watson, after Li)","Times and places where differing rationalities meet, clash and are contested or rendered technical."],
["p6","Southern theorising project","A critical perspective on place-blind Northern theory; 'southern' is a perspective, not a geography; meso-level theory rooted in place."],
["p6","Roy on informality","A mode of urbanisation; the state decides what counts as legitimate (wealthy 'informality' is legitimised, the poor are marginalised)."],
["p6","De Soto and critiques","Titling 'heroic entrepreneurs' to unlock capital. Critics: tenure security without title, titling is costly, it favours those who exploit markets."],
["p6","Insurgent planning (Miraftab)","Resistance through practice and protest; invented vs invited spaces; residents as planners."],
["p6","Langa / N2 Gateway case","A Cape Town megaproject to 'eradicate' informality at Joe Slovo settlement; the empirical test of conflicting rationalities."],
["p7","Hukou","1958 household registration (rural/urban) restricting migrants' access to urban services."],
["p7","Dual land system","State-owned urban land (tradeable use rights) vs rural collective land (must be expropriated before urban use)."],
["p7","Land-driven growth coalition","The 1994 fiscal reform left locals ~25% of tax → land-sale finance → local government + developers convert farmland."],
["p7","Authoritarian deliberation (Enning Road)","State-bounded participation: reduced conflict but limited influence, came late in the process, unequal power."],
["p7","Territorial Spatial Planning system","One system, one ministry (MNR), five levels, three plan types, three control lines, one map, a new law."],
["p7","Three control lines","Ecological protection red line, permanent basic farmland, urban development boundary."],
["p7","Fu & Zhang (2017) finding","Eco towns focus on a sustainable way of life/liveability; low-carbon towns on sustainable production; plans show intentions only."],
["p8","Hodson et al.: core argument","Urban transitions are about reconfiguring multiple innovations in context and how that is governed; innovation lies in the form of reconfiguration."],
["p8","Hodson et al.'s three themes","Experimentation–systems–spaces; multi-level urban governance; competing conceptions of sustainability."],
["p8","Transformations in / of / by cities","In: places where local–global factors drive change; of: systemic change of urban systems; by: cities' wider impacts."],
["p9","Bulkeley et al.: three ULL types","Strategic, civic, organic."],
["p9","Four ULL dispositions","Trial, enclave, demonstration, platform."],
["p9","Why co-create? (Frantzeskaki et al. 2025)","Bridges knowledge for place-based transitions, emancipates policy and planning, advances transdisciplinary research."],
["p10","Digital planning definition (Geertman & Witte)","Digital technologies and data-driven approaches to enhance the efficiency, effectiveness and inclusivity of planning."],
["p10","Risks of digital planning (Lin et al.)","Supply–demand mismatch, usability, socio-political bias, digital divide, skills gap, fragmentation, cybersecurity."],
["p11","Li & Dang's four AI risks","Deskilling, algorithmic bias, diminishing democratic engagement, blurred accountability."],
["p12","Relative vs absolute decoupling","Impact grows slower than the economy vs impact actually declines; absolute decoupling isn't evident."],
["p12","Degrowth vs post-growth (Durrant et al.)","Degrowth: structural, economic, North–South. Post-growth: a pragmatic search for alternatives within existing structures."],
["p12","Post-growth planning tools","Protect land for social/community housing, LCA and carbon accounting, maximum space standards, development ceilings, plan for shrinkage, reuse the stock, phase out infrastructure."],
["p12","Why do Durrant et al. exclude land value capture?","It relies on generating more monetised development value, which is inherently pro-growth."],
["p13","Hansen et al.: transformative or piecemeal?","Mostly incremental change; some transformation at high policy levels; limited by resources, staff and culture; justice poorly recognised."],
["p13","Frantzeskaki's seven NBS lessons (3 of them)","E.g. aesthetically appealing; create green commons; need trust in government; co-creation needs diversity; collaborative governance; inclusive mission narrative; design to learn and replicate."],
["p13","Grabowski et al. key numbers","Only 13% of US GI plans define equity; >90% lack inclusive processes; <10% identify causes of uneven vulnerability."]
];

const PSC_ESSAY=[
{t:"p3",pts:25,q:"(a) Comprehension: explain Campbell's (1996) planner's triangle and its three conflicts. (b) Essay: using the triangle, analyse green gentrification as a planning dilemma. Should planners accept it as a trade-off? Draw on at least two cases or authors from the lectures.",model:"(a) Three planning goals (economic growth, environmental protection, social equity) form a triangle. Property conflict (growth–equity), resource conflict (growth–environment), development conflict (equity–environment). Sustainable development at the centre can only be approached indirectly, through confronting conflicts; Campbell criticises sustainable development as romantic and vaguely holistic.\n(b) Green gentrification sits on the development conflict (environment vs equity), fuelled by the growth vertex (Gould & Lewis: green growth machine). Evidence: Atlanta BeltLine (+17.9–26.6%), Barcelona (location-dependent), Anguelovski et al. 2022. Position: not an inevitable trade-off (Barcelona shows it depends on context and design), so plan 'just green enough', with anti-displacement tools (CLTs, social housing), co-creation, and justice from the start (Grabowski; Frantzeskaki et al. 2025 NBS 'for whom'). A critical view: under capitalist growth dynamics (Campbell; Krähmer's green fix) greening becomes competitiveness.",rubric:["Triangle + all three conflicts named correctly","Places green gentrification on the development conflict and links it to growth","≥2 cases/authors with evidence","Clear, argued position incl. planning responses"]},
{t:"p2",pts:25,q:"(a) Comprehension: according to Angelo & Wachsmuth (2020), which three developments explain why cities came to be seen as solutions to sustainability? (b) Essay: are cities the cause of, or the solution to, unsustainability? Take a position.",model:"(a) Sprawl (North), informal settlements (South, UN-Habitat), climate change (linking both); 1970s roots, 1990s intensification; a representational shift, also environmentalists turning to cities.\n(b) Cause: ~70% of energy and CO₂, 75–80% of waste, rising DMC, inequality, growth-oriented planning, climate gentrification. Solution: agglomeration, compact form, C40, low-cost cuts in buildings and transport. Critique of the solution narrative: externalisation and consumption-based emissions (Krähmer), efficiency ≠ reduction, measurement gaps (80 cities), the post-political framing (Swyngedouw). A nuanced position: cities are sites where both play out; the question is which cities, for whom, and with or without growth (Durrant et al.; McPhearson's radical changes).",rubric:["Three roots correctly explained","Evidence on both sides","Uses a critical author (Krähmer/Swyngedouw/post-growth)","Explicit, nuanced position"]},
{t:"p6",pts:25,q:"(a) Comprehension: what do de Satgé & Watson mean by 'conflicting rationalities'? (b) Essay: can planning theories and 'best practices' developed in the Global North be transferred to cities in the Global South? Use examples from African and Chinese cities.",model:"(a) A tension between techno-managerial, modernising, marketised state planning (often allied with developers) and marginalised, often informal communities' survival rationalities, met at an 'interface'. Rationality is shaped by power (Foucault), so there is no shared public interest from which to seek consensus (vs Habermas).\n(b) Mostly no, not uncritically: colonial master planning, dual land laws and fragmentation (Maputo); apartheid used Garden City/Radburn models; the Langa N2 Gateway 'eradicating' informality; Roy on informality; de Soto's titling critique. China: communicative/collaborative planning travelled but became authoritarian deliberation (Enning Road); eco-cities via policy mobility became showcases. Nuance: a southern theorising project isn't a new binary but place-grounded, meso-level theory; ideas can travel if adapted and co-produced (Castán Broto).",rubric:["Correct definition incl. power","≥2 African examples","≥1 Chinese example of transfer/adaptation","Nuanced conclusion (not a simple binary)"]},
{t:"p7",pts:25,q:"(a) Comprehension: explain why China's spatial planning was fragmented before 2019 and what the Territorial Spatial Planning system changes. (b) Essay: can top-down sustainability experiments (eco-cities, greenways, smart cities) genuinely promote urban sustainability in China?",model:"(a) Competing bureaucracies with their own maps and priorities (city, land-use, regional, ecological, transport, function zoning); the 1994 fiscal reform → land finance → a growth coalition that bypassed protection; weak legal basis. NTSP: one system and ministry (MNR), five levels, three types, three control lines, one map, new law (Liu & Zhou).\n(b) Eco-cities (>259; Tianjin), greenways (20,000 km, often reduced to transport), smart cities (>900; scale over effectiveness, hardware over software). They stayed pilots outside statutory planning, driven by local entrepreneurialism and image (Fu & Zhang: intentions differ, outcomes unclear). They can demonstrate, but without integration into NTSP and real participation they risk isolated showcases; link to Bulkeley's demonstration/enclave dispositions and Hodson's reconfiguration.",rubric:["Causes of fragmentation incl. land finance","NTSP elements","Evidence on ≥2 experiment types","Argued judgement linking to integration/participation"]},
{t:"p12",pts:25,q:"(a) Comprehension: distinguish relative and absolute decoupling and explain why post-growth scholars doubt the latter. (b) Essay: is sustainable urban planning possible without economic growth? Discuss using Durrant et al. (2023) and at least one other course author.",model:"(a) Relative: impact grows more slowly than GDP; absolute: impact falls while GDP grows. Doubts: rebound (Jevons), problem-shifting, overestimated recycling and circularity, underestimated service-economy impacts; little evidence of absolute decoupling.\n(b) Planning is bound to growth (instruments, land value capture, monetised success; the eco-modernist green-growth label). Post-growth tools: land for social/community housing, LCA, maximum space standards, ceilings and redistribution, planning for shrinkage, reuse, phase-outs; planners voicing consequences, shared leadership. Other authors: Næss (per-capita degrowth of building stock), Daly (development without growth), Krähmer (green fix), Campbell (growth vertex), Savini et al. Counterpoints: livelihoods, recession risks, political feasibility, and Global South needs (Hickel: the North reduces).",rubric:["Correct decoupling definitions + reasons","Concrete post-growth instruments","≥1 additional author","Balanced, argued conclusion"]},
{t:"p9",pts:25,q:"(a) Comprehension: describe Bulkeley et al.'s (2019) three types of urban living labs and four dispositions. (b) Essay: what can urban experimentation and co-creation achieve for sustainability transitions, and what are their limits?",model:"(a) Strategic (national/corporate-led testing), civic (municipality-led), organic (community-led); dispositions trial, enclave, demonstration, platform.\n(b) Achievements: place-based learning, weaving knowledges, emancipating planning (Frantzeskaki et al. 2025), reconfiguring systems (Hodson), embedding experimentation as governance (Raven et al. 2026). Limits: isolated showcases/enclaves (China's eco-cities), scaling, tokenism and inclusion politics (who co-creates?), measurement, capacity (Hansen: incremental change), power (conflicting rationalities).",rubric:["Types + dispositions correct","≥2 achievements with authors","≥2 limits incl. politics/inclusion","Link to transitions/transformation"]},
{t:"p13",pts:25,q:"(a) Comprehension: what do Hansen et al. (2022) conclude about whether green space planning in European cities is transformative or piecemeal? (b) Essay: can nature-based solutions contribute to just urban transformations?",model:"(a) Mostly incremental; some deliberate transformation at high policy levels (co-governance); barriers are maintenance, funding, organisational culture and staff capacity (structural); justice and cultural diversity are poorly recognised; exnovation and multi-scale frameworks are needed.\n(b) Potential: multifunctionality, new green commons, co-creation (Frantzeskaki 2019). Risks: green gentrification (L3), inequitable plans (Grabowski: 13% define equity), NBS 'not inherently just' (Frantzeskaki et al. 2025: for whom, which nature, where); responses: transdisciplinary knowledge, justice from the start, 'just green enough'.",rubric:["Correct Hansen conclusion + barriers","NBS benefits with author","Justice risks with evidence","Conditions for just NBS"]},
{t:"p1",pts:25,q:"(a) Comprehension: explain the difference between efficiency, sufficiency and consistency strategies. (b) Essay: is 'sustainable development' too vague a concept to guide urban planning?",model:"(a) Efficiency: less resource per unit via technology (risk: rebound). Sufficiency: self-limitation, 'enough', egalitarian distribution. Consistency: qualitative change of industrial metabolism, closing cycles; Rudolf & Schmidt: efficiency and sufficiency have limited long-run effect, consistency is promising.\n(b) Vague: Daly (vague by design for consensus), Purvis (no rigorous theory), Swyngedouw (post-political, capturable), Campbell ('too vaguely holistic'). But vagueness enables coalitions; planners can make it concrete: Næss (substantive goals, per-capita limits, alliances), strong sustainability, planetary boundaries, explicit trade-offs (Campbell). Position: the vagueness is a political problem that planners must resolve by choosing and justifying substantive content.",rubric:["Three strategies correctly defined","≥2 authors on vagueness","Counter-argument (usefulness of vagueness)","Position with Næss/strong sustainability"]}
];

